/*
FOUR-TAPE UNIVERSAL TURING MACHINE
=================================

Simulates deterministic single-tape Turing machines with the tape alphabet
{0, 1, " "} and head movements L, R and S. The simulated tape is unbounded
in both directions. The simulated input contains only 0 and 1;
the empty word is also allowed.

INPUT FORMAT ON TAPE 1
---------------------
  TransitionRules#InputWord

Each rule has exactly this form:
  State,Read,NextState,Write,Move;

  State      Binary state number: 0, 1, 10, 11, 100, ...
             No leading zeros, except for the number 0 itself.
             There is no fixed limit on the length of state numbers.
  Read       0, 1 or B
  NextState  Binary state number or H, A, X
  Write      0, 1 or B
  Move       L = left, R = right, S = stay

B represents an actual blank cell " " ONLY IN THE RULES.
Only 0 and 1 may appear after #. A B in the input word is invalid.
There are no additional spaces, line breaks or start/end markers inside
the encoded input string. The first actual blank to the right of the
string marks its end. The quotation marks in the input header are not
part of the tape contents.

Rules are concatenated directly; each ends with a semicolon.
Their order is arbitrary. There must be at most one rule for each
(State, Read) pair. No separate list of states is required.
The rule list may also be empty: input: "#101" or input: "#".
A missing rule for the current configuration causes the machine to halt.

The simulated machine always starts in state 0. If necessary, rename
its states accordingly before encoding it. Its head initially points
to cell 0, the first symbol of the input word. For the empty word,
cell 0 is blank. All cells outside the input word are also blank.

Special next states (allowed only as rule targets, not as rule sources):
  H  Halt without accepting or rejecting: enter state halt.
  A  Accept and halt: enter state accept.
  X  Reject and halt: enter state reject.
The write action and head movement are performed even for H/A/X targets.
If no rule matches, the universal TM also enters halt, without changing
the simulated tape or its head position.
An infinite computation is simulated indefinitely; there is no step limit
built into this machine.

There is NO input validation. Behavior for invalid encodings is unspecified.
In particular, multiple rules for the same (State, Read) pair do not
represent supported nondeterminism.

TAPE ASSIGNMENTS
----------------
  Tape 1: Complete encoding. Read only; never modified.
  Tape 2: Actual tape of the simulated machine, containing 0, 1 and blanks.
          Its head IS the simulated head, including at negative positions.
  Tape 3: Current simulated state number starting at cell 0; blank elsewhere.
          At an explicit halt, it contains H, A or X instead.
  Tape 4: Temporary storage for the next state, also starting at cell 0.
          Completely cleared after each transfer.

Only tape 1 receives an input segment. Tapes 2, 3 and 4 start blank;
all initialization is performed by the TM rules below.

SIMULATION AND INVARIANT
-----------------------
After initialization, each simulated step begins at cycle_start.
At that point, the following invariant holds:
  - Head 1 is at cell 0, on the first rule's first symbol or on #.
  - Tape 2 and head 2 represent the simulated configuration.
  - Tape 3 contains exactly the current binary state number; head 3 = 0.
  - Tape 4 is blank; head 4 = 0.

The TM searches the rules for a matching state AND read symbol.
The entire state number must match: 1 is neither 10 nor 11.
A nonmatching rule is skipped up to its semicolon.
When a rule matches, its target is copied to tape 4, the old number on
tape 3 is completely erased, and the target is transferred from tape 4
to tape 3. Then the write action and movement are applied to tape 2,
followed by the halt decision. For a numeric next state, the search
restarts at the beginning of the program.

After initialization, only apply_move moves head 2, exactly as specified
by the encoded movement. A single simulated step generally consists of
many steps of this universal TM. Tape 2 is the output tape; its head
position is preserved when the machine halts.

EXAMPLES: REPLACE THE ENTIRE INPUT HEADER WITH ONE OF THESE
---------------------------------------------------------
1. Invert all bits up to the first blank:
   input: "0,0,0,1,R;0,1,0,0,R;0,B,H,B,S;#1010"
   Result: 0101 in cells 0..3, head at cell 4, state halt.

2. Empty input: write a 1 and accept:
   input: "0,B,A,1,S;#"
   Result: 1 in cell 0, head at cell 0, state accept.

3. Check whether the number of ones is even:
   input: "0,0,0,0,R;0,1,1,1,R;0,B,A,B,S;1,0,1,0,R;1,1,0,1,R;1,B,X,B,S;#1011"
   Result: unchanged tape 1011, head at cell 4, state reject.
   The input word 1010 reaches accept, as does the empty word.

4. Decide the language {0^n1^n | n >= 0} (the default below):
   input: "0,0,1,B,R;0,1,X,1,S;0,B,A,B,S;1,0,1,0,R;1,1,10,1,R;1,B,X,B,S;10,0,X,0,S;10,1,10,1,R;10,B,11,B,L;11,1,100,B,L;100,0,100,0,L;100,1,100,1,L;100,B,0,B,R;#000111"
   Each round erases the leftmost 0, checks that the remaining symbols
   consist of zeros followed by at least one 1, and erases the rightmost 1.
   Then the head returns to the left end of the remaining word.
   Only 0, 1 and blanks are used on the simulated tape; no extra markers.
   State roles: 0 = select a pair, 1 = scan zeros, 10 = scan ones,
   11 = erase the final 1, 100 = return to the remaining word's start.
   Result for 000111: completely blank tape, head at cell 3, state accept.
   Also accepts the empty word, 01, 0011, 00001111, etc.
   Rejects 0, 1, 001, 011, 0101, 1100, etc.
   The input is erased during matching; rejected inputs may be partly erased.

5. Infinite computation:
   input: "0,0,0,0,R;0,1,0,1,R;0,B,0,B,R;#101"
   The simulated head keeps moving to the right forever.
*/

tapes: 4
blank: " "
alphabet: {0, 1, B, H, A, X, "L", "R", "S", ",", ";", #, " "}
input: "0,0,1,B,R;0,1,X,1,S;0,B,A,B,S;1,0,1,0,R;1,1,10,1,R;1,B,X,B,S;10,0,X,0,S;10,1,10,1,R;10,B,11,B,L;11,1,100,B,L;100,0,100,0,L;100,1,100,1,L;100,B,0,B,R;#000111"
start: initialize

-- All heads start at 0. Set the simulated initial state to 0.
state initialize:
  on */*/*/* -> write same/same/0/same; move S/S/S/S; goto seek_input;

-- Scan tape 1 up to the # separator; keep the other tape heads stationary.
state seek_input:
  if t1 not in {#, " "} then move R/S/S/S;
  if t1 = # then move R/S/S/S; goto copy_input;

-- Copy the binary word to tape 2. B is not an input symbol!
-- Copying ends with both heads on the first blank to the right of the word.
state copy_input:
  if t1 = 0 then write same/0/same/same; move R/R/S/S;
  if t1 = 1 then write same/1/same/same; move R/R/S/S;
  if t1 = " " then move S/L/S/S; goto rewind_input;

-- Since the input contains only 0/1, the first blank to its left is at -1.
-- For the empty word, this phase starts directly at cell -1.
state rewind_input:
  if t2 in {0,1} then move S/L/S/S;
  if t2 = " " then move L/R/S/S; goto rewind_program;

-- Head 1 starts INSIDE the encoding, not on the blank to its right.
-- The first blank to the left is at -1. Tape 1 always remains unchanged.
state rewind_program:
  if t1 != " " then move L/S/S/S;
  if t1 = " " then move R/S/S/S; goto cycle_start;

-- Boundary between simulated steps; see the invariant in the main comment.
state cycle_start:
  on */*/*/* -> move S/S/S/S; goto candidate_start;

-- Head 1: first symbol of a candidate rule's source state, or #; head 3: cell 0.
-- # means that the entire rule list has been searched without a match.
state candidate_start:
  if t1 in {0,1} then move S/S/S/S; goto compare_state;
  if t1 = # then move S/S/S/S; goto halt;

-- Compare both numbers symbol by symbol. They match only if the comma AND
-- the blank are reached simultaneously. A proper prefix is not a match.
state compare_state:
  if (t1 = 0 and t3 = 0) or (t1 = 1 and t3 = 1) then move R/S/R/S;
  if t1 = "," and t3 = " " then move R/S/S/S; goto compare_read;
  if (t1 = 0 and t3 in {1," "}) or (t1 = 1 and t3 in {0," "}) or (t1 = "," and t3 in {0,1}) then move S/S/S/S; goto skip_rule;

-- Head 1: encoded read symbol; head 3: blank immediately after the number.
-- B on tape 1 is compared with an actual blank on tape 2.
state compare_read:
  if (t1 = 0 and t2 = 0) or (t1 = 1 and t2 = 1) or (t1 = B and t2 = " ") then move R/S/S/S; goto before_target;
  if (t1 = 0 and t2 in {1," "}) or (t1 = 1 and t2 in {0," "}) or (t1 = B and t2 in {0,1}) then move S/S/S/S; goto skip_rule;

-- Skip the current rule; leave the simulated tape unchanged.
-- Head 3 may be within its number or immediately after it.
state skip_rule:
  if t1 != ";" then move R/S/S/S;
  if t1 = ";" then move R/S/L/S; goto rewind_state_for_candidate;

-- Head 3 has already moved one cell left before entering this state.
-- This also handles a mismatch at the very first symbol of the number.
state rewind_state_for_candidate:
  if t3 in {0,1} then move S/S/L/S;
  if t3 = " " then move S/S/R/S; goto candidate_start;

-- The source state and read symbol match. Skip the second comma.
state before_target:
  if t1 = "," then move R/S/S/S; goto copy_target;

-- Copy the target to tape 4 starting at cell 0; preserve tape 3 for now.
-- On completion: head 1 -> write symbol, head 3 -> final symbol of the old ID.
state copy_target:
  if t1 = 0 then write same/same/same/0; move R/S/S/R;
  if t1 = 1 then write same/same/same/1; move R/S/S/R;
  if t1 = H then write same/same/same/H; move R/S/S/R;
  if t1 = A then write same/same/same/A; move R/S/S/R;
  if t1 = X then write same/same/same/X; move R/S/S/R;
  if t1 = "," then move R/S/L/S; goto erase_old_state;

-- Erase the entire old number, leaving no suffix if the next state is shorter.
-- Head 1 waits on the write symbol throughout the state transfer.
state erase_old_state:
  if t3 in {0,1} then write same/same/" "/same; move S/S/L/S;
  if t3 = " " then move S/S/R/L; goto rewind_target;

-- Head 3 is now at 0 and tape 3 is blank. Head 4 starts on the final target symbol.
state rewind_target:
  if t4 in {0,1,H,A,X} then move S/S/S/L;
  if t4 = " " then move S/S/S/R; goto install_target;

-- Transfer the target to tape 3 while clearing tape 4.
-- Both heads move together and finish immediately after the new ID.
state install_target:
  if t4 = 0 then write same/same/0/" "; move S/S/R/R;
  if t4 = 1 then write same/same/1/" "; move S/S/R/R;
  if t4 = H then write same/same/H/" "; move S/S/R/R;
  if t4 = A then write same/same/A/" "; move S/S/R/R;
  if t4 = X then write same/same/X/" "; move S/S/R/R;
  if t4 = " " then move S/S/L/L; goto rewind_installed_state;

-- Tape 4 is blank. Use the new ID on tape 3 to determine how far BOTH heads
-- must move left together to return to cell 0.
state rewind_installed_state:
  if t3 in {0,1,H,A,X} then move S/S/L/L;
  if t3 = " " then move S/S/R/R; goto apply_write;

-- Now overwrite the simulated tape cell; B means erase it by writing a blank.
state apply_write:
  if t1 = 0 then write same/0/same/same; move R/S/S/S; goto before_move;
  if t1 = 1 then write same/1/same/same; move R/S/S/S; goto before_move;
  if t1 = B then write same/" "/same/same; move R/S/S/S; goto before_move;

-- Skip the fourth comma and read the movement symbol.
state before_move:
  if t1 = "," then move R/S/S/S; goto apply_move;

-- Move the simulated head here. Negative positions are allowed.
state apply_move:
  if t1 = "L" then move S/L/S/S; goto dispatch_target;
  if t1 = "R" then move S/R/S/S; goto dispatch_target;
  if t1 = "S" then move S/S/S/S; goto dispatch_target;

-- BOTH writing and movement are complete. Only now may the machine halt.
-- For a numeric target, head 1 is still inside the rule that was just applied.
state dispatch_target:
  if t3 = H then move S/S/S/S; goto halt;
  if t3 = A then move S/S/S/S; goto accept;
  if t3 = X then move S/S/S/S; goto reject;
  if t3 in {0,1} then move L/S/S/S; goto rewind_program;

-- Terminal states have no outgoing rules.
state halt:

state accept:

state reject:
